To keep all your existing functionality safe while adding the new look, follow these three specific steps. I’ll show you exactly where to find the "old" code and what to replace it with.
Step 1: Update LoginStyles.js (The Style File)
Find these two functions (they are usually in the middle of the file) and replace them. Then, paste the new one at the very bottom.
Find and Replace these:
// Find BigHeading and replace with this:
export const BigHeading = styled(Typography)(({ theme }) => ({
  fontWeight: 700,
  fontSize: 28,
  lineHeight: 1.1,
  color: theme.palette.text.primary,
  marginBottom: 0, // Removed margin to close the gap
  [theme.breakpoints.down("sm")]: { fontSize: 24 },
}));

// Find SubText and replace with this:
export const SubText = styled(Typography)(({ theme }) => ({
  fontSize: 16,
  color: theme.palette.text.secondary,
  lineHeight: 1.6,
  marginTop: theme.spacing(1), // Adds a small gap before the long paragraph
}));

Paste this at the very bottom of the file:
export const SubHeadingText = styled(Typography)(({ theme }) => ({
  fontSize: "20px",    // Your requested size
  fontWeight: 500,     // Your requested weight
  color: theme.palette.text.secondary,
  lineHeight: 1.2,
  marginTop: 0,
  marginBottom: theme.spacing(1),
  display: "block",
}));

Step 2: Update the Imports in LoginPort.js
Go to the top of your LoginPort.js where you see the long list of imports from ./LoginStyles. Add SubHeadingText to that list.
import {
  Root,
  ContainerInner,
  // ... other imports
  BigHeading,
  SubHeadingText, // <--- Add this line here
  SubText,
  // ...
} from "./LoginStyles";

Step 3: Update the JSX in LoginPort.js
Scroll down to the second <LeftCol> (the one inside the second <Content> block).
Find these two lines:
<BigHeading>{content?.hero?.welcomeTitle}</BigHeading>
<SubText>{content?.hero?.subText}</SubText>

Replace ONLY those two lines with this:
{/* Grouping these in a Box overrides the large gap between them */}
<Box sx={{ mb: 1 }}>
  <BigHeading>
    {(content?.hero?.welcomeTitle || "").split(" - ")[0]}
  </BigHeading>

  {content?.hero?.welcomeTitle?.includes(" - ") && (
    <SubHeadingText>
      {content?.hero?.welcomeTitle.split(" - ")[1]}
    </SubHeadingText>
  )}
</Box>

<SubText>
  {content?.hero?.subText}
</SubText>

Why this preserves your work:
 * Safety: By only changing the code inside the second <LeftCol>, you ensure the Logo, the Right Column (Login Form), and all your API logic remain exactly as they were.
 * No Blank Screen: Using the Box wrapper and the split logic with || "" handles the text safely even if the JSON is still loading.
 * Tighter Design: The Box wrapper around the two title pieces is the key to removing that unwanted large distance.
